Revision history for SICK-Target-Proxmox

{{$NEXT}}
    - Initial release
    - Device types proxmox-ve (SICK::Target::ProxmoxVE) and proxmox-bs
      (SICK::Target::ProxmoxBS): certificate, chain and key are uploaded
      through the node's own API (POST /api2/json/nodes/<node>/certificates/
      custom); the API token is read from a file and sent only in the
      request header.
    - The connection to the node is always verified, against the roots of
      SICK's CA that the run hands the target, and the ca files and/or
      SHA-256 fingerprint pins of the device list: no system store, no
      proxy, no redirects, nothing in the environment can loosen it. A node
      that fails the check gets no request. A fresh node needs ca or a pin
      for the first upload.
    - Devices without port use 8006 (proxmox-ve) or 8007 (proxmox-bs); a
      written port, also 443 behind port forwarding, is taken as it is.
    - Key, certificate and leaf-first chain are checked before anything is
      sent; the answer must name the uploaded certificate; error messages
      never quote the token, the key or the body.
    - A device that is refused says why in a message that ends with the
      reason (a missing ca file: "No such file or directory"), not with the
      generated code the check runs in.
    - Tests run against a Perl stand-in of both APIs, including checks that
      the token does not leak.
    - Both device types ran end to end through `sick run` against a real
      PVE 9.2.21 API (pveproxy restarted through a systemctl shim, no
      systemd, no cluster) and a real PBS 4.2.8-1, each in a container with
      the lab CA (t/e2e/device-proxmox.sh, in the sick image of SICK).
      Not tried: under systemd, in a cluster, across a real network.
